<h1>
<b>Pabbajjā: Ordination</b>
</h1>
<br/>
<ul>
<li><h2 data-toc-injected="true" style="display:inline; font-size:inherit; font-weight:inherit; font-style:inherit; font-family:inherit; color:inherit; margin:0; padding:0; line-height:inherit;"><b>background</b></h2>
<ul>
<li><h3 data-toc-injected="true" style="display:inline; font-size:inherit; font-weight:inherit; font-style:inherit; font-family:inherit; color:inherit; margin:0; padding:0; line-height:inherit;">During the early period ordination (pabbajjā) and higher ordination (upasampadā) were performed concurrently. Later on the Buddha declared them as two separate disciplinary acts.</h3></li>
<li><h3 data-toc-injected="true" style="display:inline; font-size:inherit; font-weight:inherit; font-style:inherit; font-family:inherit; color:inherit; margin:0; padding:0; line-height:inherit;">Two types of monk <mark remnotemark="true" style="background-color: yellow; color: inherit;"><b>status</b></mark></h3>
<ul>
<li>sāmaṇera/ī</li>
<li>upasampadā</li>
<li>(for women there is also sikkhamāna in between sāmaṇerī and bhikkhunī)</li>
</ul>
</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><h2 data-toc-injected="true" style="display:inline; font-size:inherit; font-weight:inherit; font-style:inherit; font-family:inherit; color:inherit; margin:0; padding:0; line-height:inherit;">sāmaṇera <b>pabbajjā</b> (ordination of novices) 
            </h2><ul>
<li><h3 data-toc-injected="true" style="display:inline; font-size:inherit; font-weight:inherit; font-style:inherit; font-family:inherit; color:inherit; margin:0; padding:0; line-height:inherit;">how is the candidate for ordination called?→<b>pabbajjāpekkha</b></h3> </li>
<li><h3 data-toc-injected="true" style="display:inline; font-size:inherit; font-weight:inherit; font-style:inherit; font-family:inherit; color:inherit; margin:0; padding:0; line-height:inherit;"><b>3 main sections</b> and <b>subsections</b> of pabbajjā
                    </h3><ul>
<li>1. <mark remnotemark="true" style="background-color: transparent; color: yellow;"><b>bhaṇḍukamma / kesacchedana</b></mark> (shaving act)
                            <ul>
<li><b>informing</b> the saṅgha</li>
<li><b>shaving</b> </li>
<li><b>bathing</b> </li>
<li><b>requesting</b> ordination</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li>2. <mark remnotemark="true" style="background-color: transparent; color: yellow;"><b>kāsāvacchādana</b></mark> (wearing the robes)
                            <ul>
<li><b>giving</b> the robes to the teacher</li>
<li><b>requesting</b> the robes back</li>
<li><b>wearing</b> the robes</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li>3. <mark remnotemark="true" style="background-color: transparent; color: yellow;"><b>saraṇadāna</b></mark> (undertaking refuge)
                            <ul>
<li><b>requesting</b> refuge and precepts</li>
<li><b>giving</b> refuge and precepts</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li>after ordination: <b>upajjhāyaggāha</b> </li>
<li><b>note</b>: all 3 can be called pabbajjā</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><h3 data-toc-injected="true" style="display:inline; font-size:inherit; font-weight:inherit; font-style:inherit; font-family:inherit; color:inherit; margin:0; padding:0; line-height:inherit;"><mark remnotemark="true" style="background-color: transparent; color: yellow;"><b>who can perform</b></mark> each step of the process of pabbajjā?
                    </h3><ul>
<li><b>informing the saṅgha</b>: upasampanna or sāmaṇera following orders</li>
<li><b>bhaṇḍukamma / kesacchedana</b>: anyone, even the candidate himself but it is something that better be performed by the upasampanna</li>
<li><b>kāsāvacchādana / handing robes</b> to the pabbajjāpekkha: the preceptor himself or someone else (bhikkhu, sāmaṇera or layperson) <b>under the order of the preceptor</b> (should not be worn by oneself)
                            <ul>
<li><b>Bhikkhunā hi sahatthena vā āṇattiyā vā dinnameva kāsāvaṃ vaṭṭati, adinnaṃ na vaṭṭati</b> (mhv-a)</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><b>saraṇadāna</b>: qualified upa (10 vassa, etc. for ācariya / upajjhāya) or byatta paṭibala upa (knows the procedure well and can recite correctly) acting under his orders
                            <ul>
<li>srtdpn: saraṇadāna should be done by the preceptor or teacher
                                    <ul>
<li>pabbājetī”ti vuccati...tasmā etaṃ pabbājehīti kesacchedanaṃ kāsāyacchādanañca sandhāya vuttaṃ. </li>
</ul>
</li>
<li>vmtvndn: suggests that the 3 steps can be done by a byatta patibala upa</li>
<li>jnk: byatta paṭibala is ok when following orders of the upajjhāya</li>
<li>mhv-a: athassa upajjhāyena vā ācariyena vā “Buddhaṃ saraṇaṃ gacchāmī”tiādinā nayena saraṇāni dātabbāni yathāvuttapaṭipāṭiyāva na uppaṭipāṭiyā. </li>
<li>pabbajjācariya and upajjhāya can be the same upa or not</li>
</ul>
</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><h3 data-toc-injected="true" style="display:inline; font-size:inherit; font-weight:inherit; font-style:inherit; font-family:inherit; color:inherit; margin:0; padding:0; line-height:inherit;"><b>compulsory</b> factors for pabbajjā to be valid
                    </h3><ul>
<li>wearing <b>robes</b> having accepted them from the teacher (indirectly from a monk or sāmaṇera is also valid)</li>
<li>proper <b>recitation</b> of refuge by both the preceptor and the candidate (most basic factor)
                            <ul>
<li>If the robes are given from the candidate to the preceptor <b>indirectly</b> is it valid?→yes, it is valid </li>
</ul>
</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><h3 data-toc-injected="true" style="display:inline; font-size:inherit; font-weight:inherit; font-style:inherit; font-family:inherit; color:inherit; margin:0; padding:0; line-height:inherit;"><mark remnotemark="true" style="background-color: yellow; color: inherit;"><b>bhaṇḍukamma</b></mark></h3>
<ul>
<li>If upasampannā is involved in the bhaṇḍukamma it must be informed to the saṅgha, otherwise it is a {{44100013691564177::<b>dkṭ </b>}}offense. If a sāmaṇera or layperson does it it is not necessary but better to do so. The ideal is for upasampannas to perform bhaṇḍukamma.
                            <ul>
<li><mark remnotemark="true" style="background-color: transparent; color: purple;"><i>is there a collective term for sāmaṇeras and sāmaṇerīs? can anupasampanna be used? </i></mark><mark remnotemark="true" style="background-color: transparent; color: purple;"><i> </i></mark> #<mark remnotemark="true" style="background-color: transparent; color: purple;"><b>q</b></mark>🙋🏼‍♂</li>
<li><mark remnotemark="true" style="background-color: transparent; color: purple;"><i>what about sikkhāmānas? how is their ordination process and so on?</i></mark><mark remnotemark="true" style="background-color: transparent; color: purple;"><i> </i></mark> #<mark remnotemark="true" style="background-color: transparent; color: purple;"><b>q</b></mark>🙋🏼‍♂</li>
<li>Shouldn't the Saṅgha be informed regardless of who is performing the bhaṇḍukamma?→It is not necessary but better to do so since the purpose of the rule is that the residents come to know of a candidate being ordained. In most cases the upasampannas are involved in the kamma and the Saṅgha is informed</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><mark remnotemark="true" style="background-color: yellow; color: inherit;"><b>Informing</b></mark> the Saṅgha: 4 ways:
                            <ul>
<li>by <b>gathering</b> all the bhikkhus in the vihāra</li>
<li>by <b>going</b> to where each bhikkhu stays</li>
<li>by <b>a messenger</b> (another bhikkhu or sāmaṇera)</li>
<li>by informing only those that are within a baddha <b>sīmā</b> </li>
<li>Pāḷi for informing the saṅgha of the shaving: 
{{7929879004433074::"Saṅghaṃ bhante, imassa dārakassa bhaṇḍukammaṃ āpucchāmi"}} </li>
<li>If the candidate is shaven without prior informing of the saṅgha it is a {{2659138699757342::dkṭ offense}}
                                    <ul>
<li><mark remnotemark="true" style="background-color: transparent; color: purple;"><i>dkṭ offense for whom? upajjhāya certainly (or is it for the pabbajjācariya?) I heard all bhikkhus in the vihāra, is it true? </i></mark><mark remnotemark="true" style="background-color: transparent; color: purple;"><i> </i></mark> #<mark remnotemark="true" style="background-color: transparent; color: purple;"><b>q</b></mark>🙋🏼‍♂</li>
</ul>
</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><mark remnotemark="true" style="background-color: yellow; color: inherit;"><b>Shaving</b></mark><b> </b>
<ul>
<li>hair longer than {{2719609929156175::two finger breadths}} must be shaved. It can be done by {{2719609929156175::a bhikkhu, sāmaṇera or lay person (including the candidate himself, but it is something that better be undertaken by the bhikkhus)}} </li>
<li>It is a custom to teach the {{43903632923161284::<b>tacapañcaka</b>}} <b>kammaṭṭhāna </b>{{43903632923161284::(kesā, lomā, nakhā, dantā, taco)}} during the procedure. Ven. {{43903632923161284::Sīvali}} attained arahatship by reflecting on tacapañcaka during his bhaṇḍukamma</li>
<li>khuragga↔shaving house #[[<mark remnotemark="true" style="background-color: transparent; color: red;">pāḷi</mark> vocab (1st year)   ]] </li>
<li>Is ordination still valid if the bhaṇḍukamma is not done?→yes </li>
<li>if the candidate has hair longer than 2 finger-breadths is ordination valid?→it is valid</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><mark remnotemark="true" style="background-color: yellow; color: inherit;"><b>Bathing</b></mark>
<ul>
<li>one must become clean <b>to remove the lay odor</b> </li>
<li>A custom practiced in some monasteries: rubbing {{3190994071672111::filtered clay}} on the body, washing it off, applying {{3190994071672111::turmeric}} and washing it off again</li>
<li>If the person getting ordained is unable to bathe the teacher should bathe and clean the expectant of ordination with kindness like a father</li>
<li>pabbajjāpekkha should be dressed in clean clothes and taken to the place of ordination</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><mark remnotemark="true" style="background-color: yellow; color: inherit;"><b>Requesting Ordination</b></mark>
<ul>
<li>The candidate should pay respect to the <b>parents </b>and <b>saṅgha</b>; with <b>robes in hand</b>, <b>crouch</b> in front of the preceptor-teacher, worship him and request ordination as follows:
Pāḷi for requesting ordination with robes in hand: 
{{579926689894757::Okāsa, ahaṃ bhante, pabbajjaṃ yācāmi. (3x)}} </li>
</ul>
</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><h3 data-toc-injected="true" style="display:inline; font-size:inherit; font-weight:inherit; font-style:inherit; font-family:inherit; color:inherit; margin:0; padding:0; line-height:inherit;"><mark remnotemark="true" style="background-color: yellow; color: inherit;"><b>kāsāvacchādana</b></mark><b> (Wearing the robes)</b></h3>
<ul>
<li>Is ordination valid if refuge is taken <b>without wearing robes</b>?→no, it is a basic factor for pabbajjā to be valid</li>
<li><mark remnotemark="true" style="background-color: yellow; color: inherit;"><b>Giving</b></mark> the robes (requesting the teacher to accept the robes)
                            <ul>
<li>Pāḷi for requesting the robes to be accepted: {{7762792260373894::"(Sakalavaṭṭa-) / Sabba-dukkha-nissaraṇa-nibbāna (/nibbānassa)-sacchikaraṇatthāya imaṃ kāsāvaṃ gahetvā pabbājetha maṃ bhante, anukampaṃ upādāya (3x)}} </li>
<li>The robes <b>from the candidate’s hands </b>should be given <b>to the preceptor</b>
<ul>
<li>can pabbajjā be done in another language?→All except the saraṇadāna can be done in another language but it is traditionally done in pāḷi #<mark remnotemark="true" style="background-color: red; color: inherit;"><b>important</b></mark> </li>
</ul>
</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><mark remnotemark="true" style="background-color: yellow; color: inherit;"><b>Asking</b></mark> for the robes (requesting the teacher to give the robes)
                            <ul>
<li>Pāḷi for requesting the robes from the teacher: {{8312774372945098::"(Sakalavaṭṭa-) / Sabba-dukkha-nissaraṇa-nibbāna-(/nibbānassa) sacchikaraṇatthāya }}{{8312774372945098::<b>etaṃ</b>}}{{8312774372945098:: kāsāvaṃ }}{{8312774372945098::<b>datvā </b>}}{{8312774372945098::pabbājetha maṃ bhante, anukampaṃ upādāya (3x) }} </li>
<li>Robes should be handed back to the candidate <b>by the teacher</b> or by another bhikkhu or sāmaṇera <b>on his order</b>.</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><mark remnotemark="true" style="background-color: yellow; color: inherit;"><b>Wearing</b></mark> the robes
                            <ul>
<li>After having done <b>cīvarapaccavekkhaṇa</b> the candidate should wear the robes. The teacher or another bhikkhu or sāmaṇera on his order should assist him.</li>
<li>Obtaining the robes from the preceptor (indirectly by a bhikkhu or sāmaṇera under the orders of the teacher is also valid) is a <b>compulsory</b> matter in ordination.</li>
<li>If robes were worn before the ordination what should be done?→ordination should be done only after removing those robes and handing them over again to the candidate by monks.</li>
</ul>
</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><h3 data-toc-injected="true" style="display:inline; font-size:inherit; font-weight:inherit; font-style:inherit; font-family:inherit; color:inherit; margin:0; padding:0; line-height:inherit;"><mark remnotemark="true" style="background-color: yellow; color: inherit;"><b>saraṇadāna</b></mark><b> </b>(Giving refuge)
                    </h3><ul>
<li>(Pāḷi) <b>requesting saraṇa</b> and precepts by the pabbajjāpekkha: <i>"Okāsa, ahaṃ bhante, tisaraṇena saddhiṃ / saha pabbajjā(dasa)sīlaṃ dhammaṃ yācāmi. Anuggahaṃ katvā sīlaṃ detha me bhante (3x)  </i> </li>
<li>(Pāḷi) answer by the teacher: <i>"Yamahaṃ vadāmi taṃ vadehi / vadetha"  </i> </li>
<li>(Pāḷi) saraṇadāna by the teacher and student alternatively: <i>"Buddhaṃ saraṇaṃ gacchāmi...Dutiyampi...Tatiyampi..."   </i> </li>
<li>(Pāḷi) conclusion of the saraṇa (optional I think): <i>"Tisaraṇagamanaṁ paripuṇṇaṁ"  </i> </li>
<li>(Pāḷi) undertaking precepts (optional but usually done): <i>"Pāṇātipātā veramaṇī...adinnādānā...abrahmacariyā...musāvādā...surāmerayamajjapamādaṭṭhānā...vikālabhojanā...naccagītavāditavisūkadassanā...mālāgandhavilepanadhāraṇavibhūsanaṭṭhānā...uccāsayanamahāsayanā...jātarūparajatapaṭiggahaṇā..."  </i> </li>
<li>What are the two methods of pronunciation?→<b>Niggahītanta</b> (ending with ‘ṃ’ and <b>connecting words</b>: Buddhaṁ-saraṇaṁ-gacchāmi...) and the <b>Makāranta</b> (ending with ‘m’ - not connecting words: Buddham saraṇam gacchāmi...). It is better to do so in both ways.</li>
<li>Is saraṇadāna valid in any language and method of recitation?→For laity yes, since cetanā is the main factor. For sāmaṇeras it must be in pāḷi with proper pronunciation.</li>
<li>in samanera ordination: if the upajjhāya is pārājika it is (valid/not valid?) {{07620867115899621::still valid}}. If the pabbajjācariya is pārājika it is (valid/not valid?){{07620867115899621::not valid}}.</li>
<li>upajjhāya (preceptor) and pabbajjācariya (saraṇadāna) can also be the same bhikkhu.</li>
<li><b>"</b><mark remnotemark="true" style="background-color: yellow; color: inherit;"><i><b>yo saraṇam deti so pabbajjācariyo</b></i></mark><i><b>"</b></i> </li>
<li>can bhikkhus give saraṇadāna to sāmaṇerīs→no </li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><h3 data-toc-injected="true" style="display:inline; font-size:inherit; font-weight:inherit; font-style:inherit; font-family:inherit; color:inherit; margin:0; padding:0; line-height:inherit;">sāmaṇera<b> </b><mark remnotemark="true" style="background-color: yellow; color: inherit;"><b>sīla</b></mark></h3>
<ul>
<li>10 <b>liṅganāsana</b> (10 pārājikā for sāmaṇera)  </li>
<li>10 <b>daṇḍakammanāsana</b> (entails punishment) </li>
<li>75 <b>sekhiyā</b> </li>
<li>14 <b>duties</b> </li>
<li>Total of sāmaṇera precepts: in brief 10, in detail 109  </li>
</ul>
</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><h2 data-toc-injected="true" style="display:inline; font-size:inherit; font-weight:inherit; font-style:inherit; font-family:inherit; color:inherit; margin:0; padding:0; line-height:inherit;"><b>10 sikkhāpada</b> (10 precepts) 
            </h2><ul>
<li><h3 data-toc-injected="true" style="display:inline; font-size:inherit; font-weight:inherit; font-style:inherit; font-family:inherit; color:inherit; margin:0; padding:0; line-height:inherit;"><b>1st </b><mark remnotemark="true" style="background-color: yellow; color: inherit;"><b>pāṇātipātā </b></mark><b>veramaṇī</b> <b>sikkhāpadaṁ samādiyāmi</b></h3>
<ul>
<li>5 <b>sambhāra</b> (factors): #<mark remnotemark="true" style="background-color: red; color: inherit;"><b>important</b></mark>
<ul>
<li><b>pāṇo</b> (object is a living being)</li>
<li><b>pāṇasaññī</b> (perception of a living being)</li>
<li><b>vadhakacittaṁ</b> (intention to kill)</li>
<li><b>upakkamo</b> (means to kill)</li>
<li><mark remnotemark="true" style="background-color: transparent; color: purple;"><i><b>tena jīvitanāso</b></i></mark><mark remnotemark="true" style="background-color: transparent; color: purple;"><i> / tena maraṇaṁ (by that (effort) that life is destroyed) </i></mark> #<mark remnotemark="true" style="background-color: transparent; color: purple;"><b>q</b></mark>🙋🏼‍♂</li>
<li>Stanza:
<i>"Pāṇo have pāṇasaññī,
vadhakacittamupakkamo,
tena jīvitanāso ca, 
aṅgā pañca vadhassime."</i> </li>
</ul>
</li>
<li>If the being dies even after several years from a blow struck with the intention to kill the precept will be broken with effect from {{6497627095276219::<b>the very instant of striking</b>}}{{6497627095276219::.}}</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><h3 data-toc-injected="true" style="display:inline; font-size:inherit; font-weight:inherit; font-style:inherit; font-family:inherit; color:inherit; margin:0; padding:0; line-height:inherit;"><b>2nd</b><mark remnotemark="true" style="background-color: yellow; color: inherit;"><b> adinnādānā </b></mark><b>veramaṇī</b> <b>sikkhāpadaṁ samādiyāmi</b></h3>
<ul>
<li>5 <b>sambhāra</b> #<mark remnotemark="true" style="background-color: red; color: inherit;"><b>important</b></mark>
<ul>
<li>1. <b>parapariggahitaṃ</b> (An object that belongs to another human) </li>
<li>2. <b>parapariggahitasaññitā</b> (perceiving the object as belonging to another human)</li>
<li>3. <b>theyyacittaṃ</b> (intention to steal it)</li>
<li>4. <b>upakkamo</b> (attempt on stealing it) </li>
<li>5. <b>tena haraṇaṃ</b> (taking the object by that attempt)</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li>4 <mark remnotemark="true" style="background-color: yellow; color: inherit;"><b>exceptions</b></mark> to stealing: #<mark remnotemark="true" style="background-color: red; color: inherit;"><b>important</b></mark>
<ul>
<li><b>sakasaññā</b> (as belonging to oneself)</li>
<li><b>paṁsukūlasaññā</b> (one perceives the object as discarded, ownerless)</li>
<li><b>vissāsaggāhī </b>(taking on trust) </li>
<li><b>tāvakālika</b>saññā<b> </b>(taking temporarily)</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li>There is no freedom to take other's properties because a loss has been cause to you. If a thief steals one's property, forcing it to be returned will break the precept but {{3977863993961184::if one still perceives the property as belonging to oneself}} one may get it back</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><h3 data-toc-injected="true" style="display:inline; font-size:inherit; font-weight:inherit; font-style:inherit; font-family:inherit; color:inherit; margin:0; padding:0; line-height:inherit;"><b>3rd </b><mark remnotemark="true" style="background-color: yellow; color: inherit;"><b>abrahmacariyā </b></mark><b>veramaṇī</b> <b>sikkhāpadaṁ samādiyāmi</b></h3>
<ul>
<li><mark remnotemark="true" style="background-color: transparent; color: purple;"><i>2 </i></mark><mark remnotemark="true" style="background-color: transparent; color: purple;"><i><b>sambhāra</b></i></mark><mark remnotemark="true" style="background-color: transparent; color: purple;"><i>:</i></mark> #<mark remnotemark="true" style="background-color: transparent; color: purple;"><b>q</b></mark>🙋🏼‍♂ #<mark remnotemark="true" style="background-color: red; color: inherit;"><b>important</b></mark>
<ul>
<li>tassa pana abrahmacariyassa dve sambhārā: 
<b>sevetukāmatācittaṃ</b> (intention of having sex)</li>
<li><b>maggenamaggapaṭipatti </b>(a penis enters one of the maggas)</li>
<li><b>obs</b>: be careful here, these factors are different from the kāmesumicchācāra (akusalakammapatha) which are 4 (agamanīyavatthu, tasmiṁ sevanacittaṁ, sevanappayogo, maggenamaggapaṭipattiadhivāsanaṁ)</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li>payoga: only {{6725673053949144::sāhatthika}} </li>
<li>does masturbation break the precept?→No, but it makes the brahmacariya impure and is a daṇḍakamma vatthu</li>
