</li>
<li><b>If money is accepted by upasampanna</b>
<ul>
<li>The Buddha allowed a way for accepted money to be used for the benefit of the saṅgha righteously. The offender should relinquish the money according to the proper procedure in the presence of the saṅgha (saṅghamajjhe) and the laity can use it for offering allowable things.  </li>
<li>Kammavācā for the forfeiture of money accepted by an upasampanna. To be done among the saṅgha (incomplete): {{6286658282309467::<i>"Ahaṁ, bhante, rūpiyaṁ paṭiggahesiṁ. Idaṁ me nissaggiyaṁ. Imāhaṁ saṅghassa nissajjāmi."</i>}}{{6286658282309467:: }} </li>
<li>How should properties coming from previously accepted money be treated? (the offender accepted money, relinquished it properly and the laity used it for offering allowable things)
                                    <ul>
<li>the <b>offender cannot</b> use the requisites arranged from that money he accepted but other <b>sahadhammikas can.</b> Even though those things <b>cannot be shared</b> as personal properties. </li>
</ul>
</li>
<li>How should <b>properties bought by bhikkhus</b> be treated?
                                    <ul>
<li>Things that have been bought by the offenders cannot be used by any of the sahadhammikas ever and this cannot be corrected</li>
<li>If the offender builds a personal kuti with accepted money and then disrobes, maybe it is ok, we will discuss later. </li>
</ul>
</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li>Relinquishing the accepted money <b>without the proper procedure</b>
<ul>
<li>It is allowable to relinquish the money to a layperson but it is suggested to do so {{03397271296447202::to someone beyond the circle of kappiyakārakas to avoid expecting an invitation from the kappiyas}}. It is better to do the proper procedure because {{03397271296447202::the money can be used for the sake of the saṅgha as intended by the Buddha.}} </li>
</ul>
</li>
<li>When someone offers gold/money and keeps it in front of a monk what should be done?→the monk <b>must</b> <b>reject it</b>.  If not rejected and accepted in the mind by remaining silent the precept is <b>broken</b>  #<mark remnotemark="true" style="background-color: red; color: inherit;"><b>important</b></mark>
<ul>
<li><mark remnotemark="true" style="background-color: transparent; color: purple;"><i>If it is not rejected but not accepted in the mind the precept is not broken. (TBC)</i></mark> #<mark remnotemark="true" style="background-color: transparent; color: purple;"><b>q</b></mark>🙋🏼‍♂</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li>Does <b>touching money without accepting it</b> breaks the precept or not?→it will not break the precept but as money is considered an untouchable item it is good for sāmaṇeras to not even touch money. In the case of picking up and holding gold/money for safekeeping within the monastery intending on returning it to the owner it is allowed. #<mark remnotemark="true" style="background-color: red; color: inherit;"><b>important</b></mark> </li>
<li>How should a sāmaṇera <b>relinquish</b> money?→There is no protocol established. They can relinquish it to a lay person who better be outside of the close circle of kappiyakārakas. #<mark remnotemark="true" style="background-color: red; color: inherit;"><b>important</b></mark> </li>
<li>How to inform a layperson of the <b>presence</b> of gold/money?→It is allowable to inform the laity of the presence of some gold/money by saying: "<b>imaṁ jānāhi"</b> <b>without asking for anything</b>. The layperson can do with it as it pleases and if the layperson makes an invitation monks can ask for allowable things.</li>
<li>kappiya↔allowable (s.w. k) </li>
<li>Can the money accepted by a sāmaṇera be used by the saṅgha?→No, money cannot be used by any sahadhammikas. It should be relinquished and the sāmaṇera given daṇḍakamma.</li>
<li><mark remnotemark="true" style="background-color: transparent; color: purple;"><i>if money is accepted by </i></mark><mark remnotemark="true" style="background-color: transparent; color: purple;"><i><b>asahadhammikas</b></i></mark><mark remnotemark="true" style="background-color: transparent; color: purple;"><i> who are 10 preceptors (including sayalays) can the requisites coming from that money be accepted?</i></mark>→<mark remnotemark="true" style="background-color: transparent; color: purple;"><i>Yes. (bh. Mgvhr "but it is not ethical") </i></mark> #<mark remnotemark="true" style="background-color: transparent; color: purple;"><b>q</b></mark>🙋🏼‍♂</li>
<li><b>Pañcasahadhammika</b> (five co-religionists) #<mark remnotemark="true" style="background-color: red; color: inherit;"><b>important</b></mark>
<ul>
<li>Bhikkhu</li>
<li>Bhikkhunī</li>
<li>Sikkhamānā</li>
<li>Sāmaṇera</li>
<li>Sāmaṇerī</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li>Asahadhammika↔not one of the 5 coreligionists. </li>
</ul>
</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><h2 data-toc-injected="true" style="display:inline; font-size:inherit; font-weight:inherit; font-style:inherit; font-family:inherit; color:inherit; margin:0; padding:0; line-height:inherit;"><b>nissaya (d</b>ependence upon preceptor)    
