<li><h3 data-toc-injected="true" style="display:inline; font-size:inherit; font-weight:inherit; font-style:inherit; font-family:inherit; color:inherit; margin:0; padding:0; line-height:inherit;"><b>why</b> does the Buddha want monks to take dependence on a preceptor/teacher?
                    </h3><ul>
<li>apart from one's personal practice as a monk one must be able to <b>represent the teaching</b> properly and be able to <b>maintain the lineage</b> for the benefit of others. Therefore even arahat monks must learn under a teacher for 5 years accordingly.</li>
<li>Maghadevasutta (MN) summary - custom established by the bodhisatta of renouncing kingship after the hair was ripe. Later as a brahma he looked down and saw one king did not follow the custom and it was lost. He then took birth as a human and re-established the custom. <i>"Don't be the last monks in the sāsana. I will not come back to re-establish the lineage of monks." </i> </li>
<li><mark remnotemark="true" style="background-color: yellow; color: inherit;"><b>Even the Buddha Himself lived dependent on the Dhamma He realized</b></mark> </li>
<li><mark remnotemark="true" style="background-color: yellow; color: inherit;"><b>Well written books do not substitute a teacher</b></mark>. For the laity it may be enough for dhamma practice but not for ordained people because there are many practical things that are not in the books, because one needs corrections, because doubts may arise, because one needs examples, etc...</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><h3 data-toc-injected="true" style="display:inline; font-size:inherit; font-weight:inherit; font-style:inherit; font-family:inherit; color:inherit; margin:0; padding:0; line-height:inherit;">attitude of a mentor/pupil 
                    </h3><ul>
