<li><h2 data-toc-injected="true" style="display:inline; font-size:inherit; font-weight:inherit; font-style:inherit; font-family:inherit; color:inherit; margin:0; padding:0; line-height:inherit;"><b>nāsana</b></h2>
<ul>
<li><h3 data-toc-injected="true" style="display:inline; font-size:inherit; font-weight:inherit; font-style:inherit; font-family:inherit; color:inherit; margin:0; padding:0; line-height:inherit;">3 types of <b>nāsana</b></h3>
<ul>
<li><b>saṁvāsa</b>nāsana<b> </b>(excommunication)
                            <ul>
<li>This nāsana is related to which vinayakamma?→ukkhepanīyakamma (excommunication)  #<mark remnotemark="true" style="background-color: red; color: inherit;"><b>important</b></mark> </li>
<li><mark remnotemark="true" style="background-color: yellow; color: inherit;"><b>Ukkhepanīya</b></mark>kamma is only done against <b>bhikkhus or bhikkhunīs</b> who
                                    <ul>
<li>hold wrong views
                                            <ul>
<li>Bh. Raṭṭha (tbc) held the wrong view that sense pleasures were not a hindrance for monks because Noble lay disciples who had attained the first and second paths could have sex. What he missed was the status of a monk. 
                                                    <ul>
<li><mark remnotemark="true" style="background-color: transparent; color: purple;"><i>status of a monk is not just a concept. To be discussed.</i></mark> #<mark remnotemark="true" style="background-color: transparent; color: purple;"><b>q</b></mark>🙋🏼‍♂</li>
</ul>
</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li>repeatedly commit heavy offenses</li>
<li>or do not accept admonishment</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li>The bhikkhu / bhikkhunī who has received ukkhepanīyakamma is called {{4045422133273837::ukkhittaka.}} </li>
<li>Unless an upasampanna has {{17304528318304913::committed a pārājika}} he/she cannot be disrobed but <b>in the case of a sāmaṇera</b> it can be done. (There is the case of one bhikkhunī who was expelled by the Buddha apparently without investigation because the Buddha knew she had already {{17304528318304913::committed a pārājika offense}})</li>
<li><mark remnotemark="true" style="background-color: yellow; color: inherit;"><b>Samanubhāsana</b></mark>kamma: preliminary stages before ukkhepanīyakamma is done</li>
<li>A bhikkunī who associates ukkhittaka bhikkhus are given samanubhāsanakamma and may end up pārājika (7th prjk - ukkhittānuvattaka).</li>
<li>After the ukkhittaka bhikkhu corrects himself and the saṅgha is pleased with him another kamma is done to reinstate him as part of the saṅgha.</li>
<li><mark remnotemark="true" style="background-color: yellow; color: inherit;"><b>Pabbajanīya</b></mark>kamma: done against those who corrupt families
                                    <ul>
<li>500 monks disciples of Bh Sāriputta and Moggallāna were staying at Kitagiri (tbc) city and were corrupting families by giving gifts, riding animals, pleasing the laity, sleeping with women (but not having sex), etc. #<b>tbc</b>🕵</li>
<li>2 liṅgas of a monk
                                            <ul>
<li>shaven head</li>
<li>robe of patches</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><b>Pabbajanīyakamma</b> cannot be done against more than {{9384645983898565::3}} bhikkhus at the same time because it would be like a schism.</li>
<li><b>Fun fact</b>: Bh. Sāriputta was the upajjhāya of the chabbagiya bhikkhus as well as some of the bhikkhus who were staying in Kitagiri. This is why the Buddha sent the 2 chief disciples to deal with them.</li>
<li>After the kamma was done to those bhikkhus some disrobed. When the Buddha was informed of it He was displeased and asked: "how could they disrobe? The punishment given to them is for the purpose of correcting their conduct not for causing them to suffer."</li>
<li><mark remnotemark="true" style="background-color: yellow; color: inherit;"><b>Punishment is not to hurt but to rectify. The sāsana is based on karuṇā.</b></mark> </li>
</ul>
</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><b>liṅga</b>nāsana<b> </b>(disrobing) 
                            <ul>
<li>related to which offenses? {{1564632381327269::pārājikā}} </li>
<li>it can be done against {{7176309282282756::all sahadhammikas}} </li>
<li>If the sāmaṇera falls into pārājika, he should be {{4313049143775356::disrobed and sent away}} but if he is ready for {{4313049143775356::āyati saṁvara (future restraint)}} , he can be given ordination again.
                                    <ul>
<li>āyati saṁvara↔future discipline/restraint </li>
</ul>
</li>
<li>sāmaṇera who is pārājika technically would lose seniority. Nowadays sāmaṇeras do not pay respect to each other and line up according to age. There are different practices on this point.</li>
<li>A bhikkhu or a sāmaṇera who is pārājika and is ready for future restraint can be given the status of a {{753242884901824::sāmaṇera}}. A bhikkhunī, sikkhamāna or sāmaṇerī can be given the status of a {{753242884901824::sāmaṇerī}}. The bhikkhu and bhikkhunī cannot obtain upasampadā again but the sikkhamāna, sāmaṇera and sāmaṇerī can obtain it.</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><b>daṇḍakamma</b>nāsana (penalizations / punishments)
                            <ul>
<li>it can be done against {{9071309811111923::all sahadhammikas}} </li>
<li>related to {{5361908565465956::minor offenses}} </li>
</ul>
</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><h3 data-toc-injected="true" style="display:inline; font-size:inherit; font-weight:inherit; font-style:inherit; font-family:inherit; color:inherit; margin:0; padding:0; line-height:inherit;">nāseti↔kills, destroys (s.w. n) #[[<mark remnotemark="true" style="background-color: transparent; color: red;">pāḷi</mark> vocab (1st year)   ]] </h3></li>
