<li><h3 data-toc-injected="true" style="display:inline; font-size:inherit; font-weight:inherit; font-style:inherit; font-family:inherit; color:inherit; margin:0; padding:0; line-height:inherit;"><b>daṇḍakammas</b> (penalizations / punishments)
                    </h3><ul>
<li><b>when </b>can sāmaṇeras be penalized / punished?
                            <ul>
<li>due to committing all offenses apart from the 10 liṅganāsanas (including preliminary steps for certain liṅganāsanas), especially for committing acts prohibited by lokāvajja precepts set for bhikkhus.
                                    <ul>
<li>2 types of offenses according to 2 reasons they should be avoided #<mark remnotemark="true" style="background-color: red; color: inherit;"><b>important</b></mark>
<ul>
<li><b>loka</b>vajja: acts that are certainly done with an unwholesome mind
                                                    <ul>
<li>what about accepting money? is it lokavajja only for monks?→No, it is paṇṇattivajja. There is no difference between lokavajja/paṇṇattivajja for monks or laity</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><b>paṇṇatti / paññatti </b>vajja: should be avoided because it has been said by the Buddha</li>
</ul>
</li>
</ul>
</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><b>how </b>is the sāmaṇera's morality <b>purified</b>?
                            <ul>
<li>there is no <b>āpatti-desanā</b> for sāmaṇeras, so their morality can only be purified by undergoing daṇḍakamma.</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><b>why</b>  are penalizations given and with which mindset?
                            <ul>
<li>They are given to help the offender avoid such misconduct in the future not for hurting or causing distress. It should be done without cruelty out of compassion.</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><b>allo</b><b>wed</b><b> </b>daṇḍakammas 
                            <ul>
<li><b>āvaraṇa </b>(allowed but not used) 
                                    <ul>
<li><i>"Anujānāmi bhikkhave, āvaraṇaṃ kātuṃ”. N</i>ot allowing the offender sāmaṇera to{{42473209542298185:: go to the residence of the preceptor and the place assigned to him for residing}} 
                                            <ul>
<li><b>Gaṇṭhipada and Vimativinodaṇi</b>: only prevention of entry into <b>private</b> residence of the teacher/preceptor and of living in the Saṅgha residence the Sāmaṇera received according to his seniority.
<b>Sāratthadīpanī:</b> can be applied whether they are private residences or Saṅghika.</li>
<li>Preventing the sāmaṇera from entering the entire monastery is (allowed/not allowed) {{09659339239728615::not allowed. }}{{09659339239728615::<i>“Na bhikkhave, sabbo saṅghārāmo āvaraṇaṃ kātabbo. Yo kareyya, āpatti dukkaṭassa. Anujānāmi, bhikkave, yattha vā vasati, yattha vā paṭikkamati, tattha āvaraṇaṃ akātunti”</i>}}{{09659339239728615:: }} </li>
</ul>
</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li>what are suitable daṇḍakammas? #<mark remnotemark="true" style="background-color: red; color: inherit;"><b>important</b></mark>
<ul>
<li>carrying <b>water</b>, <b>firewood</b> or <b>sand</b> to suit the offence of the sāmaṇeras was decided as suitable penalizations in the first council (paṭhamasaṅgāyanā). Assigning works such as <b>sweeping </b>that are useful is allowed.</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li>how to mete out punishments in case of multiple offenses?
                                    <ul>
<li>In case of multiple offenses a single punishment for all offences is to be meted out.</li>
</ul>
</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><b>prohibited</b><b> </b>daṇḍakammas 
                            <ul>
<li><b>torturous punishments</b> such as {{9958072900829482::arranging to lie on a heated rock surface, getting to carry stones and timber on the head and sinking under water}} </li>
<li><b>mukhadvārika āhāra āvaraṇa</b> (preventing one from eating)
                                    <ul>
<li>the teachers who do so will be subject to {{0025791420379517316::<b>dkṭ</b>}}{{0025791420379517316::.}}Saying "{{0025791420379517316::You can eat after you have undergone this many punishments}}” is allowed but eating should be allowed irrespective of whether the punishment has been undergone or not.</li>
</ul>
</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><b>who metes out </b>daṇḍakammas 
                            <ul>
<li>It is {{11660133796036631::the preceptor}} who has the power to mete out āvaraṇa daṇḍakamma. For another upasampanna to do so without his consent is a {{11660133796036631::dkṭ. }}<i>"Na, bhikkhave, upajjhāye anāpucchā āvaraṇaṃ kātabbaṃ. Yo kareyya, āpatti </i>{{11660133796036631::<i>dukkaṭassa</i>}}<i>"</i>
<ul>
<li><mark remnotemark="true" style="background-color: transparent; color: purple;"><i>Does this apply only to āvaraṇa or to other daṇḍakammas such as sweeping as well?</i></mark><mark remnotemark="true" style="background-color: transparent; color: purple;"><i> </i></mark> #<mark remnotemark="true" style="background-color: transparent; color: purple;"><b>q</b></mark>🙋🏼‍♂</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li>If the preceptor does not punish the sāmaṇera even after a bhikkhu informs him on three occasions of a misconduct what should be done?→the sāmaṇera can be penalized by that bhikkhu</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><b>high ordination duty</b>
<ul>
<li>It is a duty of the teacher-preceptor to establish the sāmaṇeras that are properly protecting morality in higher ordination (upasampadā) at a suitable time </li>
</ul>
</li>
</ul>
</li>
</ul>
</li>
